S/61/2026-213 &amp; IFCI/CS/62/2026-214 dated March 29, 2026, to NSE and BSE respectively regarding receipt of Petition filed by Union of India through Serious fraud Investigation Office (SFIO) before the Hon’ble National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT), Principal Bench, New Delhi</in-capmkt:DetailsOfLitigationOrDisputeForWhichDisclosureIsBeingMade><in-capmkt:DateOfOriginalLitigationDisclosure contextRef="I_DevelopmentInRelationToProceedings">2026-03-29</in-capmkt:DateOfOriginalLitigationDisclosure><in-capmkt:DetailsOfDevelopmentInRelationToTheExistingProceedings contextRef="I_DevelopmentInRelationToProceedings">IFCI filed a writ petition before the Hon’ble Delhi High Court in respect of the aforementioned Petition and the Investigation Report of SFIO. 
SFIO has, inter-alia, stated in its written synopsis dated September 19, 2026 filed before the Hon’ble Delhi High Court that it is in the process of making appropriate statement before the Hon’ble NCLT seeking withdrawal of all substantive as well as interim reliefs claimed against IFCI in the Petition.
The Hon’ble Delhi High Court observed in its order dated November 17, 2026 that perusal of the aforementioned written synopsis clearly showed that the Investigation Report of SFIO and the directions issued by the Ministry of Corporate Affairs did not recommend any action, prosecution or other proceedings against IFCI Limited. Accordingly, the Hon’ble Delhi High Court has granted liberty to SFIO to approach the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) to move an appropriate application in view of the clear stand of SFIO.</in-capmkt:DetailsOfDevelopmentInRelationToTheExistingProceedings><in-capmkt:LitigationAgainstParty contextRef="I_DevelopmentInRelationToProceedings">Company</in-capmkt:LitigationAgainstParty></xbrli:xbrl>
